<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Marajoara</h1><p class="st-meta">400 CE – 1400 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Mound-building chiefdoms on Marajó Island at the mouth of the Amazon, whose elaborate painted funerary urns show that complex societies flourished in Amazonia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>From around 400 CE people on the eastern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marajo">Marajó Island</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> delta of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a>, began raising large earthen mounds called tesos above the seasonally flooded savannas. The Marajoara phase grew from earlier local pottery traditions and lasted roughly a thousand years.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The largest mounds, such as Camutins and Pacoval, held houses, cemeteries and ceremonial spaces, and some supported thousands of people. Society was probably organised in ranked chiefdoms. The economy relied on fish managed in ponds and channels, palm fruits and root crops rather than intensive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> farming. Marajoara potters made polychrome burial urns, figurines and ceramic tangas, small triangular pubic covers, decorated with incised and painted designs, many with female imagery.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Mound building ceased around 1400, before European arrival, possibly because of social change or new groups entering the island. Marajoara urns are prized museum pieces and inspired modern Brazilian decorative styles. The culture is central to debates over how many people lived in pre-Columbian Amazonia.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/marajoara.jpg" alt="Marajoara" width="320" height="427"><figcaption>Image: Daderot · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burian_urn,_AD_1000-1250,_Marajoara_culture_-_AMNH_-_DSC06177_b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>400 CE – 1400 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancestor worship</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Marajoara culture</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=900&amp;focus=marajoara" aria-label="Marajoara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3318 2629 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Marajoara lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3638 2885h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M3638 2885h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Brazil. <a href="/atlas?year=900&amp;focus=marajoara">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

